Check continuity: side 1 ends at (straight, halfDepth + 0) = (0.9, 0.8). Cap(+x) starts at a = π/2 - π/10 → x = 0.9 + cos(π/2-π/10)*0.8 = 0.9 + sin(π/10)*0.8 = 0.9+0.247=1.147, z = sin(π/2-π/10)*0.8 = 0.8*cos(18°)=0.761. Slight step from z=0.8 to 0.761 and x from 0.9 → 1.147; the polygon has a chord — acceptable (it's a polygon). Ends at a = π/2 - 9π/10 = -0.2π → x = 0.9+cos(-36°)*0.8=0.9+0.647=1.547, z = sin(-36°)*0.8=-0.47. Then side(-1) starts at (straight - 0, -(halfDepth)) = (0.9, -0.8). Hmm, mismatch: the cap ends at (1.547,-0.47) and side(-1) starts at (0.9,-0.8) → a notch. Bad.

Let me redo the polygon ordering properly: go around the perimeter as a function of angle-ish parameter:
1. Front side from x=-straight → +straight at z=+halfDepth (+bulge).
2. Right cap: from (straight, +halfDepth) clockwise to (straight, -halfDepth): angles from π/2 down to -π/2 sampled as a = π/2 - π*i/n, x = straight + cos(a)*halfDepth, z = sin(a)*halfDepth. At i=n → a=-π/2 → (straight, -halfDepth) ✓ continuous.
   My loop with i in 1..9 misses i=10 endpoint → break. Use i from 1..10 inclusive and skip duplicates later, or include endpoints and accept duplicates (ExtrudeGeometry tolerates duplicate points? It may create degenerate triangles). Cleaner: include endpoints but then the next segment starts exactly there → duplicate → remove first point of next segment.

Simplify: build each part with endpoints and dedupe consecutive duplicates at the end.

